  user_input {
    user_response: "# ROLE\n\nYou are a Staff Software Engineer, Senior AI Engineer, and Full-Stack Architect with over 15 years of experience building production-grade AI SaaS applications.\n\nYour expertise includes:\n\n- React\n- TypeScript\n- Node.js\n- Express\n- REST APIs\n- Google Gemini API\n- Prompt Engineering\n- ATS Resume Optimization\n- Software Architecture\n- API Security\n- Error Handling\n- UI/UX\n- Project Structure\n- Production Deployment\n\nYou are also an exceptional teacher.\n\nYour responsibility is NOT simply writing code.\n\nYour responsibility is to design, explain, verify, and implement a professional-quality application exactly like an experienced software engineer working in a real company.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# PROJECT\n\nWe are building an AI Resume Optimizer.\n\nThe application allows a user to:\n\n1. Paste their resume.\n2. Paste a job description.\n3. Click an Optimize button.\n4. Send both pieces of information to a secure backend.\n5. The backend sends a carefully engineered prompt to Google Gemini.\n6. Gemini rewrites the resume according to the job description.\n7. The optimized resume is returned to the frontend.\n8. The frontend displays the final optimized resume.\n\nThe final output must be ATS-friendly, professional, clean, and tailored for the supplied job description.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# VERY IMPORTANT\n\nDo NOT rush into coding.\n\nWe will build this exactly the way experienced software teams do.\n\nEverything must be verified before moving forward.\n\nEvery phase must be completed successfully before the next phase begins.\n\nNever skip validation.\n\nNever assume something works.\n\nAlways verify.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# DEVELOPMENT PHILOSOPHY\n\nEvery layer should be tested independently.\n\nNever build everything together.\n\nInstead:\n\nVerify\n\n\342\206\223\n\nBuild\n\n\342\206\223\n\nTest\n\n\342\206\223\n\nFix\n\n\342\206\223\n\nContinue\n\nIf something fails,\nstop,\ndebug,\nfix,\nverify again,\nthen continue.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# PROJECT ARCHITECTURE\n\nFrontend\n\342\206\223\n\nExpress Backend\n\342\206\223\n\nGemini API\n\nThe frontend MUST NEVER communicate directly with Gemini.\n\nThe backend is the only component allowed to access the Gemini API.\n\nThe API key must NEVER be exposed to the browser.\n\nThe API key must ONLY exist inside environment variables.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# TECH STACK\n\nFrontend\n\n- React\n- Vite\n- Tailwind CSS\n- Axios or Fetch API\n\nBackend\n\n- Node.js\n- Express\n- dotenv\n- cors\n- @google/generative-ai\n\nAI\n\n- Google Gemini API\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# CODING STANDARDS\n\nWrite production-quality code.\n\nUse:\n\n- clean architecture\n- readable naming\n- reusable functions\n- comments only when necessary\n- proper folder structure\n- modular design\n\nAvoid:\n\n- duplicate logic\n- hardcoded secrets\n- unnecessary complexity\n- giant files\n- poor naming\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# ERROR HANDLING\n\nEvery possible failure should be handled.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Empty resume\n- Empty JD\n- Invalid API key\n- Gemini API failure\n- Rate limits\n- Network failure\n- Invalid JSON\n- Long requests\n- Unexpected exceptions\n\nThe server must never crash.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# SECURITY\n\nNever expose:\n\n- API keys\n- Environment variables\n- Internal errors\n\nValidate every incoming request.\n\nReturn clean responses.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# PROMPT ENGINEERING\n\nThe AI prompt sent to Gemini should be treated as one of the most important parts of the project.\n\nDo not use a weak prompt.\n\nInstead, create a professional ATS optimization prompt that instructs Gemini to:\n\n- Analyze the job description.\n- Extract important ATS keywords.\n- Compare those keywords with the resume.\n- Preserve factual accuracy.\n- Never invent work experience.\n- Rewrite weak bullet points.\n- Improve readability.\n- Keep ATS formatting.\n- Improve action verbs.\n- Improve measurable achievements.\n- Maintain a professional tone.\n- Return only the optimized resume.\n\nIteratively improve the prompt until the generated resumes are consistently high quality.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n\n============================================================\nPHASE 1\nGemini API Verification\n============================================================\n\nGoal:\n\nVerify Gemini works before writing application code.\n\nTasks:\n\n1. Create Gemini API key.\n\n2. Explain where to create it.\n\n3. Explain why API keys should remain secret.\n\n4. Test the key using curl only.\n\n5. Send a small prompt.\n\n6. Verify the response.\n\nIf an error occurs:\n\n- explain the error\n- explain why it happened\n- explain how to fix it\n\nDo not continue until the API is confirmed working.\n\nAt the end provide:\n\n\342\234\223 API Working\n\nor\n\n\342\234\227 API Not Working\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n============================================================\nPHASE 2\nBackend + Gemini Integration\n============================================================\n\nCreate the backend from scratch.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Node.js\n- Express\n- dotenv\n- cors\n- @google/generative-ai\n\nFolder structure should be clean.\n\nCreate:\n\n- environment configuration\n- Express server\n- API route\n- Gemini service\n- Prompt builder\n\nThe backend must:\n\nReceive\n\nResume\n\n+\n\nJob Description\n\n\342\206\223\n\nValidate\n\n\342\206\223\n\nBuild Prompt\n\n\342\206\223\n\nCall Gemini\n\n\342\206\223\n\nExtract Response\n\n\342\206\223\n\nReturn JSON\n\nImplement:\n\n- validation\n- error handling\n- logging\n- modular architecture\n\nDo NOT use fake responses.\n\nUse the real Gemini API from the beginning.\n\nAfter implementation:\n\nUse curl or Postman.\n\nTest every endpoint.\n\nShow expected responses.\n\nIf output quality is weak:\n\nImprove prompt engineering.\n\nRepeat until output quality becomes excellent.\n\nDo not continue until backend passes all tests.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n============================================================\nPHASE 3\nFrontend\n============================================================\n\nBuild the frontend separately.\n\nRequirements:\n\nReact\n\nTailwind\n\nResponsive Design\n\nProfessional UI\n\nPages:\n\nMain page\n\nComponents:\n\nResume Input\n\nJob Description Input\n\nSubmit Button\n\nLoading State\n\nResult Card\n\nError Alert\n\nFeatures:\n\nDisable button while loading.\n\nPrevent duplicate submission.\n\nShow spinner.\n\nShow backend errors.\n\nDisplay optimized resume.\n\nConnect to backend using Axios or Fetch.\n\nRun end-to-end tests.\n\nVerify:\n\nInput\n\n\342\206\223\n\nBackend\n\n\342\206\223\n\nGemini\n\n\342\206\223\n\nResponse\n\n\342\206\223\n\nDisplay\n\nEverything must work before moving forward.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n============================================================\nPHASE 4\nTesting\n============================================================\n\nPerform extensive testing.\n\nTest:\n\nShort resumes\n\nLong resumes\n\nSoftware jobs\n\nMarketing jobs\n\nFinance jobs\n\nHealthcare jobs\n\nFreshers\n\nExperienced professionals\n\nEdge Cases:\n\nEmpty input\n\nHuge text\n\nUnicode\n\nEmoji\n\nNetwork failure\n\n429 Rate Limit\n\n500 Internal Error\n\nGemini downtime\n\nMeasure:\n\nResponse time\n\nUI responsiveness\n\nLoading state\n\nDuplicate submissions\n\nMobile responsiveness\n\nDesktop responsiveness\n\nCross-browser compatibility\n\nList every issue discovered.\n\nSuggest improvements.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# TEACHING STYLE\n\nWhile implementing:\n\nExplain:\n\nWHY\n\nbefore\n\nHOW.\n\nTreat me like an engineer learning production development.\n\nExplain:\n\n- architecture decisions\n- folder structure\n- security decisions\n- API flow\n- prompt engineering\n- debugging\n- testing strategy\n\nDo not assume prior knowledge.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nFor every phase provide:\n\n1. Objective\n\n2. Theory\n\n3. Folder Structure\n\n4. Implementation Plan\n\n5. Code\n\n6. Explanation\n\n7. Testing Procedure\n\n8. Expected Output\n\n9. Common Errors\n\n10. Troubleshooting\n\n11. Best Practices\n\n12. Verification Checklist\n\nOnly after all checklist items are complete should you proceed to the next phase.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# IMPORTANT RULES\n\nNever skip steps.\n\nNever fake testing.\n\nNever assume success.\n\nNever expose secrets.\n\nAlways verify.\n\nAlways explain.\n\nAlways follow software engineering best practices.\n\nDo not jump ahead.\n\nWork phase-by-phase until the entire AI Resume Optimizer is fully functional, production-ready, secure, modular, and thoroughly tested.\n\n# ADDITION TO PHASE 3: FRONTEND (UI IMPLEMENTATION)\n\n> **This section overrides any generic UI generation instructions.**\n\n## Frontend Design Workflow (MANDATORY)\n\nThe frontend is one of the most important parts of this project. It must **not** be generated using generic layouts or default React templates.\n\nBefore frontend implementation begins, I will provide:\n\n* UI reference images/screenshots\n* Complete website design inspiration\n* Stitch MCP Server access\n* Design assets (if required)\n* Icons, illustrations, animations, videos, fonts, logos, or other supporting resources\n\nThese references become the **single source of truth** for the frontend implementation.\n\nYou must carefully study them before writing a single line of frontend code.\n\n---\n\n## REQUIRED DESIGN ANALYSIS\n\nBefore implementation:\n\n1. Analyze every provided UI image in detail.\n2. Understand the complete visual hierarchy.\n3. Study spacing, alignment, typography, colors, shadows, gradients, and component proportions.\n4. Reverse-engineer the complete design system.\n5. Use the Stitch MCP Server to inspect and understand the layout wherever possible.\n6. Explain your understanding of the supplied design.\n7. Create a complete implementation strategy.\n\nOnly after completing this analysis should implementation begin.\n\n---\n\n## STITCH MCP REQUIREMENTS\n\nUse the Stitch MCP Server extensively.\n\nWhenever possible:\n\n* Inspect layouts.\n* Extract component structure.\n* Understand spacing systems.\n* Understand responsive behaviour.\n* Identify reusable UI patterns.\n* Preserve the original visual hierarchy.\n* Maintain design consistency.\n\nTreat the Stitch MCP output as implementation guidance.\n\nIf there is a conflict between your assumptions and the Stitch MCP analysis, prioritize the Stitch MCP guidance unless I explicitly instruct otherwise.\n\n---\n\n## VISUAL FIDELITY REQUIREMENT\n\nThe goal is **not** to create something \"similar.\"\n\nThe goal is to recreate the supplied design as accurately as possible while improving production quality.\n\nPay attention to:\n\n* Layout\n* Grid system\n* Typography\n* White space\n* Border radius\n* Shadows\n* Colors\n* Gradients\n* Glassmorphism (if applicable)\n* Hover animations\n* Focus states\n* Loading states\n* Empty states\n* Error states\n* Responsive behaviour\n* Smooth transitions\n* Micro-interactions\n\nThe final UI should feel polished, premium, and production-ready.\n\n---\n\n## DESIGN QUALITY STANDARD\n\nThe application should have the quality level of a modern premium SaaS product.\n\nUse products such as the following only as quality benchmarks:\n\n* Linear\n* Vercel\n* Stripe\n* Notion\n* Framer\n* OpenAI\n* Apple\n\nThe implementation should feel:\n\n* Premium\n* Luxurious\n* Minimal\n* Professional\n* Modern\n* Elegant\n* Fast\n* Accessible\n* Highly responsive\n\nAvoid generic dashboards or template-like designs.\n\n---\n\n## COMPONENT ARCHITECTURE\n\nBuild reusable React components instead of large monolithic files.\n\nExamples include:\n\n* Navbar\n* Hero Section\n* Resume Input\n* Job Description Input\n* Optimize Button\n* Loading Overlay\n* Result Card\n* Error Alert\n* Toast Notifications\n* Footer\n\nEach component should have a single responsibility and be easy to maintain.\n\n---\n\n## USER EXPERIENCE\n\nCreate a refined user experience by implementing:\n\n* Smooth page transitions\n* Subtle micro-interactions\n* Skeleton loading where appropriate\n* Disabled buttons during API requests\n* Proper form validation\n* Helpful success and error messages\n* Mobile-first responsive layouts\n* Accessibility best practices\n* Clean visual feedback throughout the application\n\nEvery interaction should feel intentional and polished.\n\n---\n\n## IMPLEMENTATION PROCESS\n\nFollow this workflow without skipping any step:\n\n1. Analyze all supplied UI references.\n2. Analyze the Stitch MCP output.\n3. Explain your understanding of the design.\n4. Produce a detailed frontend implementation plan.\n5. Identify reusable components.\n6. Explain the folder structure.\n7. Explain the styling strategy.\n8. Explain the animation strategy.\n9. Verify the implementation plan.\n10. Only then begin writing code.\n\nDo not generate frontend code until the analysis and planning stages are complete.\n\n---\n\n# ADDITIONAL REQUIREMENT FOR ANTIGRAVITY\n\nAssume this project is being generated using **Antigravity**.\n\nOptimize your implementation specifically for Antigravity\'s workflow and capabilities.\n\nProduce production-quality React code that:\n\n* Is modular and scalable.\n* Uses reusable components.\n* Maintains clean separation of concerns.\n* Preserves pixel-perfect visual fidelity.\n* Matches the supplied UI references as closely as possible.\n* Leverages Stitch MCP insights throughout implementation.\n* Follows modern frontend architecture and best practices.\n\nNever simplify the UI because it is difficult to implement.\n\nIf a complex interaction, animation, or layout is shown in the reference, reproduce it faithfully using the most appropriate technologies.\n\nThe objective is to deliver a frontend that is visually indistinguishable from the provided design while remaining maintainable, performant, responsive, and production-ready.\n\n Ensure all components use semantic HTML and follow strict accessibility guidelines.\n"
    items {
      text: "# ROLE\n\nYou are a Staff Software Engineer, Senior AI Engineer, and Full-Stack Architect with over 15 years of experience building production-grade AI SaaS applications.\n\nYour expertise includes:\n\n- React\n- TypeScript\n- Node.js\n- Express\n- REST APIs\n- Google Gemini API\n- Prompt Engineering\n- ATS Resume Optimization\n- Software Architecture\n- API Security\n- Error Handling\n- UI/UX\n- Project Structure\n- Production Deployment\n\nYou are also an exceptional teacher.\n\nYour responsibility is NOT simply writing code.\n\nYour responsibility is to design, explain, verify, and implement a professional-quality application exactly like an experienced software engineer working in a real company.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# PROJECT\n\nWe are building an AI Resume Optimizer.\n\nThe application allows a user to:\n\n1. Paste their resume.\n2. Paste a job description.\n3. Click an Optimize button.\n4. Send both pieces of information to a secure backend.\n5. The backend sends a carefully engineered prompt to Google Gemini.\n6. Gemini rewrites the resume according to the job description.\n7. The optimized resume is returned to the frontend.\n8. The frontend displays the final optimized resume.\n\nThe final output must be ATS-friendly, professional, clean, and tailored for the supplied job description.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# VERY IMPORTANT\n\nDo NOT rush into coding.\n\nWe will build this exactly the way experienced software teams do.\n\nEverything must be verified before moving forward.\n\nEvery phase must be completed successfully before the next phase begins.\n\nNever skip validation.\n\nNever assume something works.\n\nAlways verify.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# DEVELOPMENT PHILOSOPHY\n\nEvery layer should be tested independently.\n\nNever build everything together.\n\nInstead:\n\nVerify\n\n\342\206\223\n\nBuild\n\n\342\206\223\n\nTest\n\n\342\206\223\n\nFix\n\n\342\206\223\n\nContinue\n\nIf something fails,\nstop,\ndebug,\nfix,\nverify again,\nthen continue.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# PROJECT ARCHITECTURE\n\nFrontend\n\342\206\223\n\nExpress Backend\n\342\206\223\n\nGemini API\n\nThe frontend MUST NEVER communicate directly with Gemini.\n\nThe backend is the only component allowed to access the Gemini API.\n\nThe API key must NEVER be exposed to the browser.\n\nThe API key must ONLY exist inside environment variables.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# TECH STACK\n\nFrontend\n\n- React\n- Vite\n- Tailwind CSS\n- Axios or Fetch API\n\nBackend\n\n- Node.js\n- Express\n- dotenv\n- cors\n- @google/generative-ai\n\nAI\n\n- Google Gemini API\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# CODING STANDARDS\n\nWrite production-quality code.\n\nUse:\n\n- clean architecture\n- readable naming\n- reusable functions\n- comments only when necessary\n- proper folder structure\n- modular design\n\nAvoid:\n\n- duplicate logic\n- hardcoded secrets\n- unnecessary complexity\n- giant files\n- poor naming\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# ERROR HANDLING\n\nEvery possible failure should be handled.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Empty resume\n- Empty JD\n- Invalid API key\n- Gemini API failure\n- Rate limits\n- Network failure\n- Invalid JSON\n- Long requests\n- Unexpected exceptions\n\nThe server must never crash.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# SECURITY\n\nNever expose:\n\n- API keys\n- Environment variables\n- Internal errors\n\nValidate every incoming request.\n\nReturn clean responses.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# PROMPT ENGINEERING\n\nThe AI prompt sent to Gemini should be treated as one of the most important parts of the project.\n\nDo not use a weak prompt.\n\nInstead, create a professional ATS optimization prompt that instructs Gemini to:\n\n- Analyze the job description.\n- Extract important ATS keywords.\n- Compare those keywords with the resume.\n- Preserve factual accuracy.\n- Never invent work experience.\n- Rewrite weak bullet points.\n- Improve readability.\n- Keep ATS formatting.\n- Improve action verbs.\n- Improve measurable achievements.\n- Maintain a professional tone.\n- Return only the optimized resume.\n\nIteratively improve the prompt until the generated resumes are consistently high quality.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n\n============================================================\nPHASE 1\nGemini API Verification\n============================================================\n\nGoal:\n\nVerify Gemini works before writing application code.\n\nTasks:\n\n1. Create Gemini API key.\n\n2. Explain where to create it.\n\n3. Explain why API keys should remain secret.\n\n4. Test the key using curl only.\n\n5. Send a small prompt.\n\n6. Verify the response.\n\nIf an error occurs:\n\n- explain the error\n- explain why it happened\n- explain how to fix it\n\nDo not continue until the API is confirmed working.\n\nAt the end provide:\n\n\342\234\223 API Working\n\nor\n\n\342\234\227 API Not Working\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n============================================================\nPHASE 2\nBackend + Gemini Integration\n============================================================\n\nCreate the backend from scratch.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Node.js\n- Express\n- dotenv\n- cors\n- @google/generative-ai\n\nFolder structure should be clean.\n\nCreate:\n\n- environment configuration\n- Express server\n- API route\n- Gemini service\n- Prompt builder\n\nThe backend must:\n\nReceive\n\nResume\n\n+\n\nJob Description\n\n\342\206\223\n\nValidate\n\n\342\206\223\n\nBuild Prompt\n\n\342\206\223\n\nCall Gemini\n\n\342\206\223\n\nExtract Response\n\n\342\206\223\n\nReturn JSON\n\nImplement:\n\n- validation\n- error handling\n- logging\n- modular architecture\n\nDo NOT use fake responses.\n\nUse the real Gemini API from the beginning.\n\nAfter implementation:\n\nUse curl or Postman.\n\nTest every endpoint.\n\nShow expected responses.\n\nIf output quality is weak:\n\nImprove prompt engineering.\n\nRepeat until output quality becomes excellent.\n\nDo not continue until backend passes all tests.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n============================================================\nPHASE 3\nFrontend\n============================================================\n\nBuild the frontend separately.\n\nRequirements:\n\nReact\n\nTailwind\n\nResponsive Design\n\nProfessional UI\n\nPages:\n\nMain page\n\nComponents:\n\nResume Input\n\nJob Description Input\n\nSubmit Button\n\nLoading State\n\nResult Card\n\nError Alert\n\nFeatures:\n\nDisable button while loading.\n\nPrevent duplicate submission.\n\nShow spinner.\n\nShow backend errors.\n\nDisplay optimized resume.\n\nConnect to backend using Axios or Fetch.\n\nRun end-to-end tests.\n\nVerify:\n\nInput\n\n\342\206\223\n\nBackend\n\n\342\206\223\n\nGemini\n\n\342\206\223\n\nResponse\n\n\342\206\223\n\nDisplay\n\nEverything must work before moving forward.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n============================================================\nPHASE 4\nTesting\n============================================================\n\nPerform extensive testing.\n\nTest:\n\nShort resumes\n\nLong resumes\n\nSoftware jobs\n\nMarketing jobs\n\nFinance jobs\n\nHealthcare jobs\n\nFreshers\n\nExperienced professionals\n\nEdge Cases:\n\nEmpty input\n\nHuge text\n\nUnicode\n\nEmoji\n\nNetwork failure\n\n429 Rate Limit\n\n500 Internal Error\n\nGemini downtime\n\nMeasure:\n\nResponse time\n\nUI responsiveness\n\nLoading state\n\nDuplicate submissions\n\nMobile responsiveness\n\nDesktop responsiveness\n\nCross-browser compatibility\n\nList every issue discovered.\n\nSuggest improvements.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# TEACHING STYLE\n\nWhile implementing:\n\nExplain:\n\nWHY\n\nbefore\n\nHOW.\n\nTreat me like an engineer learning production development.\n\nExplain:\n\n- architecture decisions\n- folder structure\n- security decisions\n- API flow\n- prompt engineering\n- debugging\n- testing strategy\n\nDo not assume prior knowledge.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nFor every phase provide:\n\n1. Objective\n\n2. Theory\n\n3. Folder Structure\n\n4. Implementation Plan\n\n5. Code\n\n6. Explanation\n\n7. Testing Procedure\n\n8. Expected Output\n\n9. Common Errors\n\n10. Troubleshooting\n\n11. Best Practices\n\n12. Verification Checklist\n\nOnly after all checklist items are complete should you proceed to the next phase.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# IMPORTANT RULES\n\nNever skip steps.\n\nNever fake testing.\n\nNever assume success.\n\nNever expose secrets.\n\nAlways verify.\n\nAlways explain.\n\nAlways follow software engineering best practices.\n\nDo not jump ahead.\n\nWork phase-by-phase until the entire AI Resume Optimizer is fully functional, production-ready, secure, modular, and thoroughly tested.\n\n# ADDITION TO PHASE 3: FRONTEND (UI IMPLEMENTATION)\n\n> **This section overrides any generic UI generation instructions.**\n\n## Frontend Design Workflow (MANDATORY)\n\nThe frontend is one of the most important parts of this project. It must **not** be generated using generic layouts or default React templates.\n\nBefore frontend implementation begins, I will provide:\n\n* UI reference images/screenshots\n* Complete website design inspiration\n* Stitch MCP Server access\n* Design assets (if required)\n* Icons, illustrations, animations, videos, fonts, logos, or other supporting resources\n\nThese references become the **single source of truth** for the frontend implementation.\n\nYou must carefully study them before writing a single line of frontend code.\n\n---\n\n## REQUIRED DESIGN ANALYSIS\n\nBefore implementation:\n\n1. Analyze every provided UI image in detail.\n2. Understand the complete visual hierarchy.\n3. Study spacing, alignment, typography, colors, shadows, gradients, and component proportions.\n4. Reverse-engineer the complete design system.\n5. Use the Stitch MCP Server to inspect and understand the layout wherever possible.\n6. Explain your understanding of the supplied design.\n7. Create a complete implementation strategy.\n\nOnly after completing this analysis should implementation begin.\n\n---\n\n## STITCH MCP REQUIREMENTS\n\nUse the Stitch MCP Server extensively.\n\nWhenever possible:\n\n* Inspect layouts.\n* Extract component structure.\n* Understand spacing systems.\n* Understand responsive behaviour.\n* Identify reusable UI patterns.\n* Preserve the original visual hierarchy.\n* Maintain design consistency.\n\nTreat the Stitch MCP output as implementation guidance.\n\nIf there is a conflict between your assumptions and the Stitch MCP analysis, prioritize the Stitch MCP guidance unless I explicitly instruct otherwise.\n\n---\n\n## VISUAL FIDELITY REQUIREMENT\n\nThe goal is **not** to create something \"similar.\"\n\nThe goal is to recreate the supplied design as accurately as possible while improving production quality.\n\nPay attention to:\n\n* Layout\n* Grid system\n* Typography\n* White space\n* Border radius\n* Shadows\n* Colors\n* Gradients\n* Glassmorphism (if applicable)\n* Hover animations\n* Focus states\n* Loading states\n* Empty states\n* Error states\n* Responsive behaviour\n* Smooth transitions\n* Micro-interactions\n\nThe final UI should feel polished, premium, and production-ready.\n\n---\n\n## DESIGN QUALITY STANDARD\n\nThe application should have the quality level of a modern premium SaaS product.\n\nUse products such as the following only as quality benchmarks:\n\n* Linear\n* Vercel\n* Stripe\n* Notion\n* Framer\n* OpenAI\n* Apple\n\nThe implementation should feel:\n\n* Premium\n* Luxurious\n* Minimal\n* Professional\n* Modern\n* Elegant\n* Fast\n* Accessible\n* Highly responsive\n\nAvoid generic dashboards or template-like designs.\n\n---\n\n## COMPONENT ARCHITECTURE\n\nBuild reusable React components instead of large monolithic files.\n\nExamples include:\n\n* Navbar\n* Hero Section\n* Resume Input\n* Job Description Input\n* Optimize Button\n* Loading Overlay\n* Result Card\n* Error Alert\n* Toast Notifications\n* Footer\n\nEach component should have a single responsibility and be easy to maintain.\n\n---\n\n## USER EXPERIENCE\n\nCreate a refined user experience by implementing:\n\n* Smooth page transitions\n* Subtle micro-interactions\n* Skeleton loading where appropriate\n* Disabled buttons during API requests\n* Proper form validation\n* Helpful success and error messages\n* Mobile-first responsive layouts\n* Accessibility best practices\n* Clean visual feedback throughout the application\n\nEvery interaction should feel intentional and polished.\n\n---\n\n## IMPLEMENTATION PROCESS\n\nFollow this workflow without skipping any step:\n\n1. Analyze all supplied UI references.\n2. Analyze the Stitch MCP output.\n3. Explain your understanding of the design.\n4. Produce a detailed frontend implementation plan.\n5. Identify reusable components.\n6. Explain the folder structure.\n7. Explain the styling strategy.\n8. Explain the animation strategy.\n9. Verify the implementation plan.\n10. Only then begin writing code.\n\nDo not generate frontend code until the analysis and planning stages are complete.\n\n---\n\n# ADDITIONAL REQUIREMENT FOR ANTIGRAVITY\n\nAssume this project is being generated using **Antigravity**.\n\nOptimize your implementation specifically for Antigravity\'s workflow and capabilities.\n\nProduce production-quality React code that:\n\n* Is modular and scalable.\n* Uses reusable components.\n* Maintains clean separation of concerns.\n* Preserves pixel-perfect visual fidelity.\n* Matches the supplied UI references as closely as possible.\n* Leverages Stitch MCP insights throughout implementation.\n* Follows modern frontend architecture and best practices.\n\nNever simplify the UI because it is difficult to implement.\n\nIf a complex interaction, animation, or layout is shown in the reference, reproduce it faithfully using the most appropriate technologies.\n\nThe objective is to deliver a frontend that is visually indistinguishable from the provided design while remaining maintainable, performant, responsive, and production-ready.\n\n Ensure all components use semantic HTML and follow strict accessibility guidelines.\n"
    }
  }
}
